# Cursor AI Advanced Features

When to use Cursor's multi-task, worktree, and multi-root workspace features — for PasteCraft and any future project.

---

## Git Worktrees

A worktree lets multiple branches live in separate folders simultaneously. You can have `main` open in one folder and `refactor/settings-layer` open in another, both active at the same time, without stashing or branch-switching.

**When to use worktrees:**
- You need to hotfix `main` while a long refactor branch is still in progress
- Two features are being built in parallel and they touch different parts of the codebase
- You want to compare how the app behaves on two different branches side-by-side
- A CI failure on `main` needs an immediate fix and you are mid-feature

**When NOT to use worktrees:**
- The task is a single linear slice (one branch, one focus) — worktrees add overhead for no gain
- The two branches touch the same files heavily — merge conflicts become harder to manage
- You are solo and only working one task at a time

**How to create one:**
```bash
git worktree add ../PasteCraft-hotfix hotfix/login-redirect
```
This creates a sibling folder `PasteCraft-hotfix` checked out on `hotfix/login-redirect`. Both folders share the same `.git`.

**Clean up after merging:**
```bash
git worktree remove ../PasteCraft-hotfix
```

---

## Cursor Multitask Mode (Background Agents)

Multitask mode lets you run multiple Cursor agents in parallel — each working on a separate task in its own context window simultaneously.

**When to use Multitask mode:**
- Two tasks are fully independent (different files, different features)
- One task is long-running (e.g. writing tests) and you want to keep coding in the foreground
- You are exploring two different implementation approaches and want to compare results
- Running a slow analysis (CodeScene, dependency audit) while continuing to build

**When NOT to use Multitask mode:**
- Tasks share the same files — parallel agents will produce conflicting edits
- The second task depends on the output of the first — run them sequentially instead
- The task is small and fast — the overhead of spawning a second agent is not worth it

**Best pattern:**
1. Assign each agent a clearly scoped, non-overlapping slice of work
2. Use worktrees so each agent has its own branch and folder
3. Review and merge each agent's output before starting the next dependent task

