## Multi-Root Workspaces

A multi-root workspace opens multiple separate project folders in one Cursor window. Useful when a full-stack project has a frontend repo and a backend repo that are separate Git repositories.

**When to use multi-root workspaces:**
- Frontend and backend are in different repos but you work on them together (e.g. Next.js app + Supabase Edge Functions repo)
- A shared component library lives in its own repo and you need to edit it alongside the main app
- You are building a monorepo and want Cursor to index all packages together

**When NOT to use multi-root workspaces:**
- Everything is already in one repo — a single root is simpler
- The repos are unrelated — opening them together only adds noise to search and indexing

**How to set up:**
Create a `.code-workspace` file:
```json
{
  "folders": [
    { "path": "./frontend" },
    { "path": "./backend" },
    { "path": "./shared-lib" }
  ]
}
```
Open it with `File → Open Workspace from File`.

---

## Decision Table

| Situation | Tool to use |
|---|---|
| Hotfix needed while refactor is in progress | Git Worktree |
| Two independent features building in parallel | Worktree + Multitask |
| Frontend + backend in separate repos | Multi-Root Workspace |
| Long analysis running, want to keep coding | Multitask Mode |
| Single linear task, one branch | None — keep it simple |
| Same files being changed by two tasks | Sequential only, no parallel agents |

---

## Applied to PasteCraft

- **Refactor slices** (Settings, PDF, Auth) are sequential — no worktrees needed unless a hotfix interrupts
- **If a production bug appears mid-refactor:** create a worktree for the hotfix, fix it, merge to `main`, then return to the refactor branch
- **If the website and extension ever split into separate repos:** use a multi-root workspace to keep both open together
- **Future SaaS projects with Next.js frontend + Supabase backend in separate repos:** multi-root workspace from day one
